Z||000006||MrPBody||03-31-2001||10:41 AM||||"Better" for what purpose?  Constant-rate may be better for pure racing; progressive-rate may be a better compromise for street performance.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||03-31-2001||01:05 PM||||I'd have to agree that fixed rate springs are definately better for racing and autocross.  I do believe; however, that progressive rate springs are better for the typical street car.  You can get a more compliant ride while still having better handling than if the spring was a fixed rate compliant spring. Also, a fixed rate stiff spring may handle better, but it'll ride much worse.  Also, don't forget that the typical road isn't as well kept as a race track, so the progressive spring is more forgiving.  Just my opinion.  You mileage may vary.  Contents packed by weight, not volume.  Some settling may have occurred during shipping.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000008||danny boy||03-31-2001||01:31 PM|||| Here's my theory. Progressive rate springs using coilovers I think would be benificial. Ride height aside, Say you adjusted so they fit snug.That would give you normal working rates. the tighter they were tensioned, the stiffer they would get.Solid rate springs would need to be changed whenever a different rate was desired.Of course this would be a seat of the pants type testing, but thats the only way I can do stuff. ||||reg||1
